Why exceptions.

Every method so far has quietly assumed the happy path — the file exists, the number parses, the list isn't empty. But real programs live in a world where those assumptions break. And the question every language must answer is this: when something goes wrong deep inside a call, how does the code that can fix it find out? Java's answer is exceptions — a channel for error information that is separate from a method's normal return value, and that cannot be silently ignored.

Consider the old way, and why it rots. Before exceptions, the standard trick was to make failure part of the return value — a special sentinel, or an error code the caller was trusted to check. So you parse the input, and if the result is minus one, that's supposed to mean an error. But two things rot here. First, the error and the real answer share one channel — so a valid minus one is indistinguishable from a failure. And second, worse, nothing forces the check. Forget the if, and the bad value flows on, corrupting everything downstream until it fails somewhere far from the cause. The signal and the recovery drift apart, and that's exactly what makes such bugs miserable to trace.

The exception model fixes this by splitting error reporting off from the return value entirely. When a method hits a situation it can't handle, it throws — it abandons its normal flow, and hands an exception object up the call stack. The runtime unwinds method after method, each one abandoned, until it finds a catch that's prepared to handle that type — or the program stops.

So picture a try block. Inside it, you call integer-dot-parse-int, which may throw a number-format-exception, and then you process the number. If the parse throws, that process call is skipped entirely, and control jumps to the catch, which handles the bad input. The return value carries only the real answer; the error travels its own path. And here's the key virtue: an unhandled exception doesn't quietly continue. It stops the program with a report. A failure is loud and immediate, not a corrupt value drifting downstream.

When an exception does go unhandled, Java prints a stack trace — the exact chain of method calls that led to the failure, innermost first. It names the exception, it shows the input that caused it, and it lists each method and line, from the deepest frame back up to main. That trace is the single most useful debugging artifact in Java. It tells you the failure, the input, and the precise line — so you read down to the deepest frame in your own code, and you start there. Learning to read a stack trace is learning to debug Java.

So here's what this module covers. First the model: the throwable hierarchy, and the crucial split between checked and unchecked exceptions. Then the try, catch, and finally mechanics. Then throwing your own, and writing custom exception types. Then chaining, so a low-level cause isn't lost. Then the payoff for resources — try-with-resources, for guaranteed cleanup. And that leads naturally into the second half of the module: files and input-output, the canonical place where things go wrong, handled with the modern path and files API. The throughline: make failure explicit, handle it where you can, and never let it pass silently.
